  {
   "id": "fn-p003-1",
   "page": 3,
   "mark": "2",
   "text": "This terminology is adapted from [25], where Weyl writes about the “enveloping algebra” of a group representation as the algebra generated by the endomorphisms on the representing space coming from the action of all group elements. In modern terminology, this is just the image of the representation’s linear extension to the group algebra.",
   "marks": [
    {
     "page": 3,
     "context": "alsobyτ.Theenvelope"
    }
   ],
   "block": "library/arxiv-math-0610591v3/blocks/prose-sec-001"
  },
  {
   "id": "fn-p004-1",
   "page": 4,
   "mark": "3",
   "text": "Thenotionof“polynomial”functionsongenerallineargroupsgoesback(at least) to Schur’s 1901 dissertation.",
